  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-34277",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216822/assets/e3abf66be20a5ab3ed5d0b8812b127d6336cb88f8369937d415c8918d020e45e.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{5}{4})}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={225}^{∘}.$",
